-- README for HackedBox 0.8.x --

HackedBox is a lightweight XLibre window manager derived from the
original Blackbox window manager. The 0.8.x series modernizes the original
codebase while retaining its lightweight design, traditional resource-based
configuration, and minimal desktop dependencies.

HackedBox is developed for Unix-like systems using the X11 protocol and is
currently developed and tested with XLibre on Linux.


Source Tree:
------------

The HackedBox source tree is organized into the following directories:

    data/       - Default styles, backgrounds, menus, and other installed
                  data files.

    doc/        - Documentation and manual pages.

	hbSettings  - Start of a built in settings handler.

    hbtk/       - The HackedBox Toolkit (HbTK), the native GUI toolkit being
                  developed for HackedBox and related applications.

	include/    - The Hackedbox window manager header files.

    src/        - The HackedBox window manager source code.



Build Requirements:
-------------------

See the DEPENDENCIES file for the complete list of required libraries,
development headers, and build tools.

The dependency list is maintained separately so that it can be kept
up to date as the HackedBox source tree changes.


Quick Start:
------------

The quickest way to build and install HackedBox is to run:

    ./bootstrap
    ./configure --prefix=/usr
    make -j$(nproc)
    sudo make install

For available configuration and build options, run:

    ./configure --help

Additional information about the Autotools build system is available in
the INSTALL file.


Installation:
-------------

The default installation prefix is /usr when configured with:

    ./configure --prefix=/usr

HackedBox installs the window manager and included utilities into the
system binary directory and installs its data files under:

    /usr/share/hackedbox/

Installed data includes:

    /usr/share/hackedbox/styles/
    /usr/share/hackedbox/backgrounds/
    /usr/share/hackedbox/menu


Configuration:
-------------

HackedBox uses a traditional resource-based style system for configuring
the appearance of the window manager.

Styles control items such as:

    - menu appearance
    - window decorations
    - fonts
    - colors
    - gradients
    - borders
    - button appearance
    - handles and grips
    - menu bullets
    - background images

The installed styles are located in:

    /usr/share/hackedbox/styles/

The Default style is installed as the initial style for new HackedBox
installations. HackedBox also includes a collection of additional styles.

User configuration and custom styles are stored under:

    ~/.hackedbox/


Backgrounds:
-----------

HackedBox includes the hbsetbg utility for setting the X root background.

Styles configure the background using:

    root.background
    root.background.mode

For example:

    root.background: /usr/share/hackedbox/backgrounds/default.jpg
    root.background.mode: center

User backgrounds may be stored in:

    ~/.hackedbox/backgrounds/

See the hbsetbg documentation for the available background modes and
options.

The older rootCommand is no longer compatable with 0.8.8 and newer Hackedbox styles

Styles:
-------

The Default style is intended to provide the standard HackedBox appearance
on a new installation.

Additional styles are provided as examples and alternatives. Users can
copy an existing style and modify it to create their own appearance.

For information about the style resource format and available settings,
see:

    data/README.style


Menus:
------

HackedBox uses a configurable menu system. The installed default menu is
provided in:

    /usr/share/hackedbox/menu

Menu configuration and syntax are documented in:

    data/README.menu


Utilities:
---------

HackedBox currently includes:

    hbsetbg

hbsetbg is the HackedBox background-setting utility. It supports multiple
image placement modes and is designed to operate with multi-screen X
displays.

The utility is built as part of HackedBox and does not require an external
background-setting program.


HbTK:
-----

HbTK is the HackedBox Toolkit, a native C++ GUI toolkit being developed
alongside HackedBox.

HbTK provides the foundation for reusable GUI components and applications
without requiring GTK, Qt, or another large external GUI toolkit.

The HbTK source is located in:

    hbtk/

HbTK is an ongoing part of the HackedBox project and is being developed
independently of the existing HackedBox window manager code where practical.


Compatibility:
--------------

HackedBox is currently developed and tested on:

    Linux x86_64
    XLibre

Additional platforms being tested:

    FreeBSD x86_64
    
The 0.8.x codebase is derived from an older Blackbox codebase that supported
a number of additional platforms. Those historical platforms are not
currently part of the primary HackedBox development or testing environment.


Documentation:
--------------

Additional documentation is available throughout the source tree.

    INSTALL
        Build and installation information.

    DEPENDENCIES
        Required libraries, development packages, and build tools.

    data/README
        Information about the installed HackedBox data files.

    data/README.menu
        Menu configuration and syntax.

    data/README.style
        Style configuration and available resources.

    doc/
        HackedBox documentation and manual pages.
    
    doc/README.setup
    	Updated set up and configuration pages.


Reporting Problems:
-------------------

When reporting a build or runtime problem, include the HackedBox version,
operating system, architecture, configure options, and any relevant error
messages or build output.


